1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,250
Imagine waking up one morning and
discovering that almost everyone

2
00:00:04,300 --> 00:00:06,210
in your village is gone.

3
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:12,490
No fire, no war, no visible
disaster, just an eerie silence.

4
00:00:13,160 --> 00:00:18,030
Now imagine discovering that the thing
responsible for all of this was a lake,

5
00:00:18,280 --> 00:00:22,830
a beautiful, peaceful lake that people
had lived beside for generations.

6
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:25,790
This might sound like
something from a horror movie,

7
00:00:25,840 --> 00:00:28,930
but on August 21st, 1986,

8
00:00:29,100 --> 00:00:31,850
something extraordinary
happened in Cameroon.

9
00:00:32,200 --> 00:00:35,150
A lake released a deadly cloud of gas.

10
00:00:35,520 --> 00:00:39,110
More than 1,700 people lost their lives,

11
00:00:39,280 --> 00:00:43,510
thousands of animals were killed, and
scientists were left investigating one

12
00:00:43,560 --> 00:00:47,110
of the strangest natural
disasters in modern history.

13
00:00:47,300 --> 00:00:51,570
But how could something as harmless looking
as a lake cause so much destruction?

14
00:00:51,620 --> 00:00:55,450
And perhaps more importantly,
could it happen again?

15
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:58,810
This is the story of Lake Nyos.

16
00:00:59,340 --> 00:01:00,340
File One.

17
00:01:00,520 --> 00:01:02,010
The Unknown File.

18
00:01:02,760 --> 00:01:06,730
Our story begins in northwestern
Cameroon, in central Africa.

19
00:01:07,020 --> 00:01:11,690
Surrounded by beautiful green mountains
lies a small volcanic crater lake known

20
00:01:11,740 --> 00:01:13,310
as Lake Nyos.

21
00:01:13,580 --> 00:01:16,550
From above, it looks like
something out of a postcard.

22
00:01:16,940 --> 00:01:21,350
Calm water, steep green hills,
and peaceful rural communities.

23
00:01:21,560 --> 00:01:23,810
Nothing about its appearance
would make you suspect

24
00:01:23,860 --> 00:01:26,990
that something deadly was
hiding beneath the surface.

25
00:01:27,520 --> 00:01:30,870
For generations, people had lived
in the surrounding valleys.

26
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:36,450
The land was fertile, and nearby communities
depended on the landscape for their livelihoods.

27
00:01:36,500 --> 00:01:40,230
But deep beneath the lake,
something unusual was happening,

28
00:01:40,440 --> 00:01:42,930
something nobody could
see from the surface.

29
00:01:43,440 --> 00:01:47,970
Carbon dioxide from geological sources
beneath the lake was slowly entering

30
00:01:48,020 --> 00:01:53,210
its deepest waters, and year after
year, that gas was accumulating.

31
00:01:53,840 --> 00:01:56,750
Now, carbon dioxide itself isn't unusual.

32
00:01:56,940 --> 00:01:59,330
It's a gas we produce
every time we breathe.

33
00:01:59,520 --> 00:02:01,470
It's also present in the atmosphere.

34
00:02:01,860 --> 00:02:03,990
But in very high concentrations,

35
00:02:04,160 --> 00:02:07,010
carbon dioxide can be extremely dangerous,

36
00:02:07,060 --> 00:02:10,870
and Lake Nyos was storing
enormous amounts of it.

37
00:02:11,140 --> 00:02:13,250
To understand what happened next, you need

38
00:02:13,300 --> 00:02:16,830
to understand one extraordinary
scientific detail.

39
00:02:17,180 --> 00:02:20,710
The deeper you go underwater, the
greater the pressure becomes,

40
00:02:20,760 --> 00:02:26,490
and under that pressure, large amounts of
carbon dioxide can remain dissolved in water.

41
00:02:26,840 --> 00:02:29,370
Think of a sealed bottle
of sparkling water.

42
00:02:29,480 --> 00:02:33,870
When the bottle is closed, the carbon
dioxide stays dissolved under pressure.

43
00:02:33,920 --> 00:02:37,930
But when you open it, the
pressure drops, and suddenly,

44
00:02:37,980 --> 00:02:39,410
bubbles begin escaping.

45
00:02:39,860 --> 00:02:43,790
Now imagine something similar
happening inside an entire lake,

46
00:02:43,840 --> 00:02:48,770
except instead of a refreshing drink,
you're dealing with a massive reservoir

47
00:02:48,820 --> 00:02:50,310
of trapped gas.

48
00:02:50,540 --> 00:02:54,810
For years, Lake Nyos continued
accumulating carbon dioxide,

49
00:02:54,920 --> 00:02:58,270
until one evening in August 1986.

50
00:02:58,740 --> 00:03:03,970
On August 21st, ordinary life was continuing
in villages surrounding the lake.

51
00:03:04,100 --> 00:03:06,170
People were going about their routines.

52
00:03:06,360 --> 00:03:09,890
The lake was still there, surrounded
by the same familiar hills.

53
00:03:09,940 --> 00:03:11,830
But deep underwater,

54
00:03:12,140 --> 00:03:14,030
something was about to change.

55
00:03:14,240 --> 00:03:19,730
That evening, an enormous amount of carbon
dioxide suddenly escaped from Lake Nyos.

56
00:03:19,880 --> 00:03:24,210
Gas-rich water had moved upward,
allowing dissolved gas to break free

57
00:03:24,260 --> 00:03:25,870
as the pressure decreased.

58
00:03:26,060 --> 00:03:30,350
The release created a dangerous cloud
that spread outward from the lake.

59
00:03:30,400 --> 00:03:35,310
And because carbon dioxide is denser than
ordinary air under comparable conditions,

60
00:03:35,360 --> 00:03:39,950
concentrated gas could flow downhill and
collect in the surrounding valleys.

61
00:03:40,040 --> 00:03:44,070
It moved through the landscape,
reaching populated communities.

62
00:03:44,300 --> 00:03:45,810
Imagine the danger.

63
00:03:45,900 --> 00:03:48,790
You cannot see the carbon dioxide itself.

64
00:03:48,920 --> 00:03:52,810
You cannot escape it simply by
closing your eyes or turning away.

65
00:03:52,860 --> 00:03:55,450
And when the concentration
becomes high enough,

66
00:03:55,500 --> 00:03:59,090
the air around you can no longer
support normal breathing.

67
00:03:59,360 --> 00:04:03,590
In the villages below Lake Nyos, people
and animals were suddenly exposed

68
00:04:03,640 --> 00:04:06,130
to deadly concentrations of gas.

69
00:04:06,360 --> 00:04:08,090
Some people lost consciousness.

70
00:04:08,380 --> 00:04:10,050
Many never recovered.

71
00:04:10,260 --> 00:04:12,710
Thousands of livestock also died.

72
00:04:12,840 --> 00:04:15,850
By the time the scale of the
disaster became clear,

73
00:04:15,900 --> 00:04:22,410
more than 1,700 human lives had been lost,
entire communities had been devastated,

74
00:04:22,460 --> 00:04:25,970
and surviving families
faced unimaginable grief.

75
00:04:26,140 --> 00:04:30,890
But at first, the precise explanation
for the disaster was far from obvious.

76
00:04:31,140 --> 00:04:33,770
Scientists needed to
determine what had happened.

77
00:04:33,880 --> 00:04:35,690
Was this a volcanic eruption?

78
00:04:35,820 --> 00:04:38,310
Had something toxic
escaped from the ground?

79
00:04:38,400 --> 00:04:42,010
Was there a previously unknown
natural phenomenon at work?

80
00:04:42,220 --> 00:04:44,390
Investigators traveled to the region.

81
00:04:44,440 --> 00:04:45,750
They studied the water.

82
00:04:45,800 --> 00:04:47,270
They collected samples.

83
00:04:47,340 --> 00:04:50,910
They examined the effects of the
disaster on the surrounding landscape.

84
00:04:51,060 --> 00:04:54,990
And gradually, the evidence pointed
toward a remarkable explanation.

85
00:04:55,040 --> 00:04:58,630
The lake had released an enormous
quantity of carbon dioxide.

86
00:04:58,680 --> 00:05:01,890
The gas had accumulated deep underwater.

87
00:05:02,140 --> 00:05:06,010
Then, during a sudden release, it
escaped into the atmosphere.

88
00:05:06,300 --> 00:05:10,770
Researchers came to understand this
type of disaster as a limnic eruption,

89
00:05:10,900 --> 00:05:13,890
an extremely unusual event
in which large amounts

90
00:05:13,940 --> 00:05:17,070
of dissolved gas are suddenly
released from a lake.

91
00:05:17,140 --> 00:05:20,570
And this explanation revealed
something deeply unsettling.

92
00:05:20,760 --> 00:05:24,610
The danger had been building for
years, hidden beneath a surface

93
00:05:24,660 --> 00:05:26,730
that looked perfectly peaceful.

94
00:05:26,800 --> 00:05:29,310
But one major question remained.

95
00:05:29,540 --> 00:05:33,090
Why had the gas suddenly escaped
on that particular night?

96
00:05:33,540 --> 00:05:36,590
Scientists examined several
possible explanations.

97
00:05:37,020 --> 00:05:41,110
Some researchers considered whether a
landslide could have disturbed the water.

98
00:05:41,320 --> 00:05:45,990
Others investigated the possibility of
mixing between different layers of the lake.

99
00:05:46,320 --> 00:05:48,070
What scientists understood was

100
00:05:48,120 --> 00:05:52,610
that bringing gas-rich deep water
upward could start a chain reaction.

101
00:05:52,660 --> 00:05:55,270
As the water rose, the pressure dropped.

102
00:05:55,320 --> 00:05:58,850
Dissolved carbon dioxide
escaped as bubbles.

103
00:05:59,100 --> 00:06:02,450
Those bubbles could then help
bring even more water upward,

104
00:06:02,500 --> 00:06:08,370
releasing even more gas, until an enormous
quantity of carbon dioxide had escaped.

105
00:06:08,640 --> 00:06:12,710
But the exact initial trigger at
Lake Nyos has remained uncertain.

106
00:06:12,900 --> 00:06:14,850
And that's an important distinction.

107
00:06:15,160 --> 00:06:19,290
Scientists developed a strong explanation
for how the disaster happened,

108
00:06:19,340 --> 00:06:23,690
but identifying precisely what started
the process is more difficult.

109
00:06:24,040 --> 00:06:27,670
However, there was another
disturbing part of this story.

110
00:06:27,780 --> 00:06:30,690
Because Lake Nyos wasn't
the first warning.

111
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:36,110
Two years earlier, something remarkably similar
had happened at another lake in Cameroon.

112
00:06:36,300 --> 00:06:38,390
Its name was Lake Monoun.

113
00:06:38,740 --> 00:06:43,810
On August 15, 1984, a deadly
gas release occurred there.

114
00:06:43,980 --> 00:06:45,930
37 people died.

115
00:06:46,380 --> 00:06:49,990
Scientists investigating Lake Monoun
began studying the possibility

116
00:06:50,040 --> 00:06:55,090
that carbon dioxide had accumulated
underwater before escaping suddenly.

117
00:06:55,260 --> 00:06:58,850
It was an unusual and poorly
understood natural hazard,

118
00:06:58,960 --> 00:07:02,790
one that was only beginning to
receive serious scientific attention.

119
00:07:03,120 --> 00:07:08,170
Then, two years later, Lake Nyos
experienced a much larger disaster,

120
00:07:08,220 --> 00:07:10,850
and the consequences were devastating.

121
00:07:11,220 --> 00:07:15,130
The events transformed how researchers
understood the potential dangers

122
00:07:15,180 --> 00:07:17,550
of certain volcanic crater lakes.

123
00:07:17,880 --> 00:07:20,730
But understanding what
happened wasn't enough.

124
00:07:20,820 --> 00:07:22,830
There was another urgent problem.

125
00:07:22,980 --> 00:07:27,490
If carbon dioxide could slowly
accumulate inside Lake Nyos once,

126
00:07:27,600 --> 00:07:29,770
what would prevent it
from happening again?

127
00:07:30,220 --> 00:07:34,130
Scientists continued investigating the
lake, and their observations revealed

128
00:07:34,180 --> 00:07:37,910
that carbon dioxide was still
entering its deep waters.

129
00:07:38,020 --> 00:07:42,410
The disaster hadn't permanently removed
the underlying source of the problem.

130
00:07:42,560 --> 00:07:45,670
Over time, gas could accumulate again.

131
00:07:46,060 --> 00:07:49,630
Researchers needed to find a way
to release that gas safely,

132
00:07:49,680 --> 00:07:52,650
without allowing another
catastrophic build-up.

133
00:07:52,840 --> 00:07:56,530
The solution they developed was
surprisingly simple in concept.

134
00:07:56,740 --> 00:07:57,740
Pipes.

135
00:07:57,820 --> 00:08:00,790
Specially designed pipes
extending into the deep,

136
00:08:00,840 --> 00:08:01,970
gas-rich water.

137
00:08:02,020 --> 00:08:04,770
When deep water was brought
upward through a pipe,

138
00:08:04,820 --> 00:08:06,250
the pressure decreased.

139
00:08:06,560 --> 00:08:10,510
This allowed dissolved carbon dioxide
to escape in a controlled way.

140
00:08:10,560 --> 00:08:15,410
Instead of waiting for the lake to release
a massive quantity of gas suddenly,

141
00:08:15,500 --> 00:08:17,930
engineers could gradually remove it.

142
00:08:18,120 --> 00:08:23,290
In 2001, a permanent degassing
system began operating at Lake Nyos.

143
00:08:23,500 --> 00:08:26,030
Additional pipes were installed in 2011.

144
00:08:26,340 --> 00:08:30,930
The system helped greatly reduce the
amount of dangerous gas stored underwater.

145
00:08:31,200 --> 00:08:33,630
It was a remarkable example of scientists

146
00:08:33,680 --> 00:08:37,110
and engineers using their
understanding of a natural disaster

147
00:08:37,160 --> 00:08:39,050
to help prevent another one.

148
00:08:39,100 --> 00:08:42,230
But the story doesn't end
with the engineering.

149
00:08:42,480 --> 00:08:45,770
Because Lake Nyos teaches us
something much bigger,

150
00:08:46,060 --> 00:08:49,730
not every natural disaster
comes with an obvious warning.

151
00:08:50,040 --> 00:08:53,990
Some of the most dangerous
geological processes happen slowly,

152
00:08:54,240 --> 00:08:57,990
silently, far beyond
what our eyes can see.

153
00:08:58,060 --> 00:08:59,630
And sometimes, a place

154
00:08:59,680 --> 00:09:04,410
that looks completely ordinary can
conceal an extraordinary hazard.

155
00:09:04,700 --> 00:09:09,450
Today, photographs of Lake Nyos
still show a beautiful landscape.

156
00:09:09,720 --> 00:09:10,870
Green mountains.

157
00:09:11,040 --> 00:09:12,150
Still water.

158
00:09:12,320 --> 00:09:14,070
A peaceful crater lake.

159
00:09:14,340 --> 00:09:18,190
If you didn't know its history, you might
never imagine what happened there on

160
00:09:18,240 --> 00:09:19,510
that August night.

161
00:09:19,820 --> 00:09:23,470
More than 1,700 people lost their lives.

162
00:09:23,800 --> 00:09:26,570
Families and communities
were forever changed.

163
00:09:26,620 --> 00:09:27,950
And a disaster that

164
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:32,670
once seemed almost impossible helped
scientists understand a previously

165
00:09:32,720 --> 00:09:35,170
underappreciated natural danger.

166
00:09:35,540 --> 00:09:37,050
The lake is still there.

167
00:09:37,160 --> 00:09:38,850
The hills are still there.

168
00:09:38,900 --> 00:09:43,730
But now, the world understands something
it didn't fully understand before.

169
00:09:44,120 --> 00:09:45,070
Sometimes,

170
00:09:45,120 --> 00:09:48,310
the greatest danger isn't
something you can see approaching.

171
00:09:48,520 --> 00:09:51,910
It's something that's been building
silently beneath the surface.

172
00:09:51,960 --> 00:09:54,910
This was File One,

173
00:09:55,100 --> 00:09:56,550
The Unknown File.

